This peer proves that the fixed points of an inflationary retraction form a finitary domain, with compact fixed points obtained by closing principal elements.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`ClosureOperator`).** An approximable endomap is a closure operator when it is a retraction and lies above the identity map.

**Definition (`IsCompactFix`).** A fixed point is compact among fixed points when, whenever it lies below the directed supremum of a family of fixed points, it already lies below one member of that family.

**Definition (`CompactFix`).** A compact fixed point is a fixed point equipped with the assertion that it is compact among fixed points.

**Definition (`botFix`).** The least fixed point of a closure operator is the closure of the least element.

**Theorem (`botFix_le`).** The least fixed point lies below every fixed point.

*Proof.* Monotonicity sends the least element below the closure of any fixed point, which equals that fixed point. ∎

**Theorem (`isCompactFix_botFix`).** The least fixed point is compact among fixed points.

*Proof.* In any nonempty directed family it lies below every member, hence below a chosen member. ∎

**Definition (`botK`).** The bottom compact fixed point is the least fixed point together with its compactness.

**Theorem (`le_toElementMap_of_isClosureOperator`).** Every element lies below its image under a closure operator.

*Proof.* The closure operator lies above the identity, and map order is evaluated pointwise on elements. ∎

**Theorem (`isCompactFix_sup`).** If two compact fixed points have a common upper bound, then the closure of their bounded supremum is a compact fixed point.

*Proof.* Below a directed supremum, each compact point lies below some family member; directedness gives one member above both, and monotonicity of closure places the closed supremum below that fixed member. ∎

**Theorem (`isCompactFix_toElementMap_principal`).** The closure of every principal element is a compact fixed point.

*Proof.* Inflationarity reduces a bound on its closure to a bound on the principal element; compactness of principal elements chooses one family member, and monotonicity of closure completes the comparison. ∎

**Definition (`genK`).** A basic neighbourhood determines the compact fixed point obtained by closing its principal element.

**Definition (`joinCompactFix`).** Two compact fixed points below a common fixed point are joined by closing their bounded supremum.

**Theorem (`joinCompactFix_le`).** The joined compact fixed point remains below the chosen common fixed upper bound.

*Proof.* The bounded supremum lies below the upper bound, and closure is monotone while the upper bound is fixed. ∎

**Definition (`upFix`).** The upper neighbourhood generated by a compact fixed point consists of all compact fixed points above it.

**Theorem (`mem_upFix`).** Membership in an upper neighbourhood is exactly domination of its generating compact fixed point.

*Proof.* This is the defining condition. ∎

**Theorem (`self_mem_upFix`).** A compact fixed point belongs to its own upper neighbourhood.

*Proof.* The order is reflexive. ∎

**Theorem (`upFix_antitone`).** If one compact fixed point lies below another, the upper neighbourhood of the latter is contained in that of the former.

*Proof.* This follows by transitivity of the order. ∎

**Theorem (`upFix_injOn`).** Equal upper neighbourhoods have generators with equal underlying fixed points.

*Proof.* Each generator belongs to its own upper neighbourhood; equality yields both order inequalities, hence antisymmetry. ∎

**Theorem (`upFix_inter`).** For two compact fixed points with a common fixed upper bound, the intersection of their upper neighbourhoods is the upper neighbourhood of their join.

*Proof.* A compact fixed point lies above both generators exactly when it lies above their bounded supremum and therefore above its closure; the reverse implication uses inflationarity and the inclusion of each generator in the supremum. ∎

**Theorem (`upFix_subset_upFix_botK`).** Every upper neighbourhood is contained in the upper neighbourhood generated by the bottom compact fixed point.

*Proof.* The bottom fixed point lies below every fixed point. ∎

**Definition (`domainOfClosure`).** The domain of a closure operator is the neighbourhood system whose tokens are compact fixed points and whose basic neighbourhoods are their upper neighbourhoods.

**Definition (`confirmedIdx`).** For an element of the closure domain, a confirmed index is a compact fixed point whose upper neighbourhood belongs to that element.

**Definition (`instNonemptyConfirmedIdx`).** Every closure-domain element has a confirmed index.

*Proof.* Its master neighbourhood is the upper neighbourhood of the bottom compact fixed point. ∎

**Theorem (`confirmedIdx_directed`).** The underlying fixed points of the confirmed indices of an element form a directed family.

*Proof.* The element confirms the intersection of two confirmed upper neighbourhoods; a basic upper neighbourhood below that intersection provides a confirmed compact fixed point above both generators. ∎

**Definition (`fromElementFilter`).** A closure-domain element determines the directed supremum of the underlying fixed points of all its confirmed indices.

**Definition (`genFamily`).** For a fixed point and one of its neighbourhoods, the generated fixed point is the closure of the corresponding principal element.

**Theorem (`genFamily_directed`).** The generated fixed points associated with the neighbourhoods of a fixed point form a directed family.

*Proof.* Principal elements associated with an element's neighbourhoods form a directed family, and monotonicity of closure preserves the two required inequalities. ∎

**Definition (`toElementFilter`).** A fixed point determines the closure-domain element that confirms exactly those upper neighbourhoods whose compact generators lie below it.

**Theorem (`le_of_toElementFilter_le`).** If the closure-domain element associated with one fixed point lies below that associated with another, then the first fixed point lies below the second.

*Proof.* Test each neighbourhood of the first point using the compact closure of its principal element. Equality of generators for equal upper neighbourhoods and inflationarity show that the neighbourhood also belongs to the second point. ∎

**Theorem (`toElementFilter_fromElementFilter`).** Sending a closure-domain element to its fixed point and then back to a filter returns the original element.

*Proof.* Compactness turns a bound below the directed supremum into a bound below one confirmed index; antitonicity then gives membership. The converse follows from the canonical inclusion of a confirmed index in the supremum. ∎

**Theorem (`toElementFilter_mono`).** The filter assigned to a fixed point is monotone in that fixed point.

*Proof.* Any compact generator below the smaller fixed point is below the larger one by transitivity. ∎

**Theorem (`toElementMap_eq_iSupDirected_genFamily`).** Every fixed point is the directed supremum of the closures of the principal elements determined by its neighbourhoods.

*Proof.* Express the underlying element as the directed supremum of its principal approximants, use preservation of directed suprema by the map, and then use fixedness. ∎

**Theorem (`fromElementFilter_toElementFilter`).** Sending a fixed point to its filter and reconstructing a fixed point returns the original fixed point.

*Proof.* Every confirmed compact generator lies below the fixed point, while the generated principal family is cofinal in the reconstruction and has supremum equal to the fixed point. ∎

**Definition (`fixSetOrderIso`).** The fixed points of a closure operator are order-isomorphic to the elements of its closure domain.

**Theorem (`isCompactFix_iff`).** A fixed point is compact among fixed points exactly when it is the closure of a principal element arising from some neighbourhood.

*Proof.* Compactness selects one member from the directed generated family whose supremum is the fixed point, and the reverse inequality follows from monotonicity and fixedness. The converse is compactness of closed principal elements. ∎

**Theorem (`isFinitary_of_isClosureOperator`).** Every closure operator is finitary.

*Proof.* Its compact fixed points form the closure domain, whose elements are order-isomorphic to all fixed points. ∎

**Theorem (`example_814`).** Compact fixed points of a closure operator are exactly closures of principal elements, and every closure operator is finitary.

*Proof.* Combine the compact-fixed-point characterization with the finitarity theorem. ∎
